9 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186184232 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186185205 #edb/ref 1061 461792 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186185205 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "3f1ff0dc-f8c4-5e2b-90b2-8f6efe37b016" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186185205 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186185300 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186185300 #edb/ref 1064 "Consider the quadratic equation\n\n$$\nax^2+bx+c = 0,\n$$\n\nwhere $a\\neq 0.$ We can derive a general solution to this equation by completing the square.\n\nFirst, let's subtract $c$ from both sides to isolate the variable terms:\n\n$$\nax^2+bx = -c\n$$\n\nSince $a\\neq 0,$ dividing by $a$ gives\n\n$$\nx^2 + \\dfrac{b}{a}x = -\\dfrac{c}{a}.\n$$\n\nNext, we complete the square on the left-hand side and move the constant terms to the right-hand side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+2\\cdot \\frac{b}{2a}x=-\\frac{c}{a} \\\\ {(x+\\frac{b}{2a})}^{2}-{(\\frac{b}{2a})}^{2} & =-\\frac{c}{a} \\\\ {(x+\\frac{b}{2a})}^{2}-\\frac{{b}^{2}}{4{a}^{2}} & =-\\frac{c}{a} \\\\ {(x+\\frac{b}{2a})}^{2} & =\\frac{{b}^{2}}{4{a}^{2}}-\\frac{c}{a}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nPutting the terms on the right-hand side over a common factor of $4a^2,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\left(x + \\dfrac{b}{2a}\\right)^2  = \\dfrac{b^2 - 4ac}{4a^2}.\n$$\n\nTaking the square root of both sides yields\n\n$$\nx + \\dfrac{b}{2a}  = \\pm \\sqrt{\\dfrac{b^2 - 4ac}{4a^2}}\n$$\n\nwhich can be simplified as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+\\frac{b}{2a} & =\\pm \\frac{\\sqrt{{b}^{2}-4ac}}{2a}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we isolate $x{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =-\\frac{b}{2a}\\pm \\frac{\\sqrt{{b}^{2}-4ac}}{2a}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich simplifies as\n\n$$\nx = \\dfrac{-b \\pm \\sqrt{b^2 - 4ac}}{2a}.\n$$\n\nThis formula is called the **quadratic formula**. You'll learn more about how to apply the quadratic formula in future lessons." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186185300 #edb/ref 1065 13276 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186185300 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "cc56cc55-b423-5e0b-aed0-e07bdb86f874" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186185300 #edb/ref 1067 "Deriving the Quadratic Formula" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186185352 #edb/ref 1064 "We can use the method of completing the square to solve quadratic equations with leading coefficients. \n\nAs an example, let's solve the following quadratic equation by completing the square:\n\n$$\n4x^2-8x+3 = 0\n$$\n\nFirst, we move the constant term to the right-hand side and complete the square on the left-hand side.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{x}^{2}-8x+3 & =0 \\\\ 4{x}^{2}-8x & =-3 \\\\ 4({x}^{2}-2x) & =-3 \\\\ 4({x}^{2}-2\\cdot 1\\cdot x+{1}^{2}-{1}^{2}) & =-3 \\\\ 4[(x-1)^{2}-1] & =-3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, expanding the brackets, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4[(x-1)^{2}-1] & =-3 \\\\ 4(x-1)^{2}-4 & =-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we isolate the perfect square by adding  $4$ to both sides and then dividing by $4\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4(x-1)^{2}-4 & =-3 \\\\ 4(x-1)^{2}-4+4 & =-3+4 \\\\ 4(x-1)^{2} & =1 \\\\ (x-1)^{2} & =\\frac{1}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we solve the equation using the square root method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-1)^{2} & =\\frac{1}{4} \\\\ \\sqrt{(x-1)^{2}} & =\\sqrt{\\frac{1}{4}} \\\\ |x-1| & =\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ x-1 & =\\pm \\frac{1}{2} \\\\ x & =1\\pm \\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we simplify the solutions:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & =1-\\frac{1}{2}=\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ {x}_{2} & =1+\\frac{1}{2}=\\frac{3}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solutions are $x=\\dfrac12$ and $x=\\dfrac32.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186185352 #edb/ref 1065 6214 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186185352 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "7d202804-803f-5139-b32a-fca689609abb" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186185352 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186244375 #edb/ref 1080 "p+q=5" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186244375 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186244375 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "07967e46-3d53-5076-933f-f82b5f556405" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186244875 #edb/ref 1080 "p+q=\\frac{7}{6}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186244875 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186244875 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "401d3db9-46c6-5718-9d5d-157bd289930b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186246162 #edb/ref 1080 "p+q=\\frac{25}{6}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186246162 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186246162 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "ced9c051-dab3-5f30-85db-fb6e73c83a5d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186246419 #edb/ref 1080 "p+q=6" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186246419 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186246419 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "ee4131a8-2bd5-5dc8-8936-3564d8e553a0" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186246453 #edb/ref 1080 "p+q=\\frac{7}{3}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186246453 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186246453 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "f25b295c-3860-584d-b014-b08e0d6fae63" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186246820 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186244375 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186246820 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186244875 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186246820 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186246162 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186246820 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186246419 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186246820 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186246453 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186246820 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186246820 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186246820 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186246419 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186246820 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "9c061393-35f4-5189-baec-26f62e17b0dd" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186247291 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186246820 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186247291 #edb/ref 1069 "q-112589" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186247291 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186247291 #edb/ref 1071 "The equation $6{z}^{2}+10z-1=0$ can be written in the form $6(z+p)^{2}=q.$ Find $p+q.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186247291 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "165c4c89-9a6c-555e-8270-074787fb6a50" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186247291 #edb/ref 1074 "First, we move the constant term to the right-hand side, factor the coefficient of the quadratic term, and complete the square on the left-hand side.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6{z}^{2}+10z-1 & =0 \\\\ 6{z}^{2}+10z & =1 \\\\ 6({z}^{2}+\\frac{5}{3}z) & =1 \\\\ 6[{z}^{2}+2\\cdot \\frac{5}{6}\\cdot z+{(\\frac{5}{6})}^{2}-{(\\frac{5}{6})}^{2}] & =1 \\\\ 6[{(z+\\frac{5}{6})}^{2}-{(\\frac{5}{6})}^{2}] & =1 \\\\ 6[{(z+\\frac{5}{6})}^{2}-\\frac{25}{36}] & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we expand the brackets, and write the equation in the required form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6{(z+\\frac{5}{6})}^{2}-6\\cdot \\frac{25}{36} & =1 \\\\ 6{(z+\\frac{5}{6})}^{2}-\\frac{25}{6} & =1 \\\\ 6{(z+\\frac{5}{6})}^{2} & =\\frac{31}{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $p=\\frac{5}{6}$ and $q=\\frac{31}{6}.$ Finally,\n\n$$\np+q=\\frac{5}{6}+\\frac{31}{6}=\\frac{36}{6}=6.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186264254 #edb/ref 1080 "p+q=\\frac{11}{4},\\,z=\\frac{3\\pm \\sqrt{11}}{2}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186264254 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186264254 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "92714716-5bbe-5cee-94c5-d4a5659903a3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186264411 #edb/ref 1080 "p+q=\\frac{15}{4},\\,z=\\frac{1\\pm \\sqrt{11}}{2}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186264411 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186264411 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "a5141912-69d1-5f4a-bca0-9d7248df1949" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186264761 #edb/ref 1080 "p+q=\\frac{15}{4},\\,z=\\frac{-1\\pm \\sqrt{13}}{2}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186264761 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186264761 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "c8fa13dc-c910-5b24-a8c9-a978ec6e8a33" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186264974 #edb/ref 1080 "p+q=\\frac{13}{4},\\,z=\\frac{-1\\pm \\sqrt{13}}{2}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186264974 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186264974 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "e33d3856-1005-5557-b459-f0f8f8c83489" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186264980 #edb/ref 1080 "p+q=\\frac{17}{4},\\,z=\\frac{-3\\pm \\sqrt{11}}{2}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186264980 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186264980 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "e3b99594-5bf2-5660-9213-d4ace307273e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186265570 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186264254 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186265570 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186264411 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186265570 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186264761 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186265570 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186264974 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186265570 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186264980 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186265570 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186265570 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186265570 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186264761 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186265570 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "b8ab3eaf-e5a0-5af1-8071-252570846a31" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186265780 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186265570 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186265780 #edb/ref 1069 "q-12983" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186265780 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186265780 #edb/ref 1071 "The equation $4{z}^{2}+4z=12$ can be written in the form $(z+p)^{2}=q.$ Find $p+q,$ and solve the equation." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186265780 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "e3fdc752-039f-5f05-8441-586cbafa095b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186265780 #edb/ref 1074 "First, we move the constant term to the right-hand side, divide it by the coefficient of the quadratic term, and complete the square on the left-hand side.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{z}^{2}+4z & =12 \\\\ 4({z}^{2}+z) & =12 \\\\ {z}^{2}+z & =3 \\\\ {z}^{2}+2\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}\\cdot z & =3 \\\\ {z}^{2}+2\\cdot (\\frac{1}{2})\\cdot z+{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}-{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{2} & =3 \\\\ {(z+\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}-{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{2} & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we isolate the perfect square:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(z+\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}-{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{2} & =3 \\\\ {(z+\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}-\\frac{1}{4} & =\\frac{12}{4} \\\\ {(z+\\frac{1}{2})}^{2} & =\\frac{13}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $p=\\frac{1}{2},q=\\frac{13}{4},$ and $p+q=\\frac{15}{4}.$ \n\nNext, we solve the equation using the square-root method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{{(z+\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}} & =\\sqrt{\\frac{13}{4}} \\\\ |z+\\frac{1}{2}| & =\\frac{\\sqrt{13}}{2} \\\\ z+\\frac{1}{2} & =\\pm \\frac{\\sqrt{13}}{2} \\\\ z & =-\\frac{1}{2}\\pm \\frac{\\sqrt{13}}{2} \\\\ z & =\\frac{-1\\pm \\sqrt{13}}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186330121 #edb/ref 1080 "p=-\\frac{21}{4},\\,x=\\frac{3\\pm \\sqrt{21}}{4}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186330121 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186330121 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "15778872-121f-5b94-8744-9a4c9cf32f4e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186330350 #edb/ref 1080 "p=-\\frac{3}{4},\\,x=\\frac{6\\pm \\sqrt{21}}{4}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186330350 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186330350 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "2d3dfa53-4d0c-5bb3-9a4c-bd1ad30b1391" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186331006 #edb/ref 1080 "p=\\frac{21}{4},\\,x=\\frac{6\\pm \\sqrt{21}}{4}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186331006 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186331006 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "759b0a96-8ac6-5393-b1ad-e344cf880c0b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186331403 #edb/ref 1080 "p=\\frac{3}{4},\\,x=\\frac{3\\pm \\sqrt{21}}{4}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186331403 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186331403 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "a18fbe3